\title{AMD-Agent: Autonomous Malware Detection Agent}

\author{Baruh Ifraimov \and Dor Cohen}

\date{\today}



\begin{document}

\maketitle



\begin{abstract}

This project presents AMD-Agent, an autonomous LLM-assisted continual-learning system for PE malware detection. The agent collects malware and benign samples from live sources, validates downloaded binaries, extracts static PE features, monitors concept drift, and retrains a LightGBM classifier using replay-based learning. A local Ollama-based LLM agent supports the workflow by assisting with source selection, CTI filtering, PE error triage, and drift explanations, while deterministic fallbacks keep the system operational when the LLM is unavailable. To evaluate updates reliably, AMD-Agent compares the previous and updated models on a strict temporal holdout excluded from training, reporting Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and False Positive Rate (FPR). The result is an adaptive malware detection pipeline that combines autonomous collection, LLM-assisted decision making, continual retraining, and measurable before/after evaluation.

\end{abstract}



\section{Introduction}



Malware detection is a continuously changing problem. New malware variants, packers, and evasion techniques appear over time, while benign software also changes through new versions and build processes. A classifier trained once on a fixed dataset may therefore become outdated when deployed in a live environment. This motivates the need for continual learning and reliable update evaluation.



AMD-Agent addresses this problem by implementing an autonomous pipeline for PE malware detection. The system discovers malware and benign samples from external sources, downloads and validates PE files, extracts static features, monitors drift, performs inference, and retrains the model when new labeled data or drift evidence is available. The classifier is based on LightGBM with feature selection and FPR-aware threshold calibration.



A key part of the project is avoiding misleading evaluation. For each model update, AMD-Agent creates a strict chronological holdout and excludes it from retraining. The previous and updated models are then evaluated on the same holdout using Accuracy, Precision, Recall, and FPR. This enables a direct before/after comparison while reducing data leakage.



The system also includes optional local LLM support through Ollama for source selection, CTI filtering, and drift explanations. The LLM is not the malware classifier; it assists the agent’s workflow while deterministic fallbacks keep the system operational. Overall, AMD-Agent demonstrates a practical approach for adaptive malware detection with automated collection, retraining, and evaluation.



\section{Related Work}



This section reviews the papers and tools that directly influenced AMD-Agent. 

For each item, we summarize the methodology or purpose, its strengths and limitations, a short comparison to our work, and how it contributed to our system design. 

Table~\ref{tab:related_work_tools} provides a consolidated overview.



\subsection{pefile}

\textit{pefile} is a Python library for static parsing of Windows Portable Executable (PE) files. 

It exposes PE headers, sections, imports, exports, resources, timestamps, data directories, and other metadata without executing the binary. 

This makes it useful for malware-analysis pipelines where samples must be inspected safely.



\textbf{Strengths:} Mature and widely used; simple Python API; safe static parsing; exposes many PE structures needed for malware feature extraction.



\textbf{Limitations:} Static-only; cannot observe runtime behavior; packed, corrupted, or intentionally malformed binaries may produce incomplete parsing results.



\textbf{Comparison:} Unlike dynamic analysis tools, \textit{pefile} does not execute malware or collect behavioral traces. 

This matches our design choice to build a lightweight and safer static-analysis pipeline.



\textbf{Contribution:} We use \textit{pefile} in the FeatureExtraction stage to extract headers, section statistics, imports, exports, data directories, timestamps, entropy-related metadata, and PE structural features.



\subsection{EMBER}

Anderson and Roth~\cite{anderson2018ember} introduce EMBER, a well-known dataset and feature representation for static PE malware classification. 

EMBER includes malware-specific features such as byte histograms, byte-entropy histograms, strings, imports, exports, headers, and section information. 

The original work also provides a reproducible baseline using LightGBM.



\textbf{Strengths:} Strong malware-specific feature baseline; reproducible; practical for large-scale static classification; works well with tabular ML models.



\textbf{Limitations:} Static features can be evaded through packing, obfuscation, or adversarial manipulation. 

The original EMBER feature vector is fixed, while our project uses a custom EMBER-like representation.



\textbf{Comparison:} EMBER focuses on a benchmark dataset and static feature representation. 

AMD-Agent uses a similar static-feature philosophy, but applies it in a live continual-learning pipeline with automatic collection, drift detection, and retraining.



\textbf{Contribution:} EMBER directly inspired our 2304-dimensional EMBER-like static PE feature vector, including byte histograms, byte-entropy features, strings, imports, exports, headers, and section metadata.



\subsection{MalwareBazaar API}

MalwareBazaar is a public malware-sharing platform operated by abuse.ch. 

It provides an API for querying malware metadata, filtering samples by file type, checking SHA256 hashes, and downloading malware samples. 

It is useful for systems that require fresh real-world malware instead of only fixed benchmark datasets.



\textbf{Strengths:} Live real-world malware source; metadata-rich; supports SHA256 lookup and sample download; well aligned with autonomous malware-collection workflows.



\textbf{Limitations:} API quotas and availability issues; samples may disappear or fail to download; metadata may be noisy; malware handling requires strict sandboxing and validation.



\textbf{Comparison:} Unlike static datasets such as EMBER, MalwareBazaar provides a live data stream. 

This makes it more realistic for continual learning, but also less stable and harder to control experimentally.



\textbf{Contribution:} MalwareBazaar is our main malware acquisition source. 

AMD-Agent uses it in SourceDiscovery and BinaryFetch for PE discovery, metadata collection, and sandboxed malware download.



\subsection{River and ADWIN}

River is an online machine-learning library for streaming data. 

One relevant component is ADWIN, an adaptive windowing algorithm for detecting changes in data streams. 

ADWIN maintains a dynamic window of recent observations and detects statistically significant differences between older and newer parts of the stream.



\textbf{Strengths:} Lightweight; suitable for online and continual-learning settings; provides tested streaming drift-detection algorithms; does not require storing the entire stream.



\textbf{Limitations:} Drift detection depends on the monitored signal and threshold configuration. 

A single-feature detector can miss complex multivariate changes, while overly sensitive settings may cause unnecessary retraining.



\textbf{Comparison:} ADWIN gives a statistical drift signal, but does not explain the semantic reason for drift. 

AMD-Agent combines ADWIN with multivariate feature-shift checks and optional LLM-based drift explanations.



\textbf{Contribution:} River/ADWIN is used in DriftMonitor over section entropy, together with rolling multivariate feature-shift checks, to decide when retraining may be needed.



\subsection{MADAR}

Rahman et al.~\cite{rahman2025madar} propose MADAR, a continual-learning method for malware analysis based on diversity-aware replay. 

The goal is to reduce catastrophic forgetting by replaying selected historical samples when training on new malware data. 

This is important because malware distributions change over time and new families or campaigns can dominate recent samples.



\textbf{Strengths:} Designed for malware continual learning; replay helps preserve older knowledge; diversity-aware selection is more suitable than naive random replay.



\textbf{Limitations:} Requires stored historical samples or extracted features; replay quality depends on label quality and sample diversity; replay alone does not prevent evaluation leakage.



\textbf{Comparison:} MADAR focuses on the retraining strategy. 

AMD-Agent adopts the replay idea but adds strict temporal holdout exclusion so that model-update evaluation does not reuse samples seen during training.



\textbf{Contribution:} MADAR inspired the ModelRetrain component. 

We use replay samples together with newly labeled samples to retrain the LightGBM classifier while reducing catastrophic forgetting.



\subsection{TESSERACT}

Pendlebury et al.~\cite{pendlebury2019tesseract} propose TESSERACT, an evaluation methodology for malware classification that reduces temporal and spatial bias. 

The main argument is that random train-test splits can overestimate performance because similar samples from the same period or source may appear in both training and testing. 

TESSERACT instead evaluates models using chronological splits.



\textbf{Strengths:} More realistic than random splits; explicitly addresses temporal bias; useful for evaluating robustness under concept drift.



\textbf{Limitations:} Requires enough labeled samples in each chronological split. 

When validation or test data contains only one class, metrics such as FPR or Recall become unreliable and evaluation may need to be skipped.



\textbf{Comparison:} TESSERACT is an evaluation methodology, not a retraining method. 

AMD-Agent uses TESSERACT-style evaluation as a robustness view, while using a separate strict holdout comparison for before/after production model updates.



\textbf{Contribution:} TESSERACT inspired our chronological evaluation design, our use of Accuracy, Precision, Recall, FPR, and AUT, and our decision to skip temporal evaluation when splits are single-class.



