}

Area Under Time (AUT) summarizes recovery across retraining iterations when multiple evaluation runs are available.



\subsection{False Positives and False Negatives}

A critical analysis of the model's errors reveals that False Positives (benign files flagged as malicious) are heavily penalized by our threshold constraints. False Negatives (malware bypassing detection) primarily occur when novel packing routines emerge, masking traditional static indicators. Retraining directly targets these FN spikes by folding the novel features into the active model.



\subsection{Evolution of Sources}

Over extended runs, the agent's data acquisition evolves. Initial bootstrapping relies heavily on MalwareBazaar. As API quotas are reached or specific sources experience downtime, the \texttt{SourceSelector} dynamically routes to secondary APIs (MalShare, ThreatFox) and utilizes web-based CTI scraping to maintain steady ingestion rates.



\subsection{Model Improvements (Before vs. After Retraining)}

Drift cycles are documented in \texttt{drift\_log.jsonl}. By capturing pre-drift and post-drift metrics, the evaluation node demonstrates measurable improvements in accuracy and recall immediately following a retraining event, validating the continual learning architecture.



\section{Advanced Implementations (Bonus)}

\begin{itemize}

    \item \textbf{LLM-Based Content Analysis:} Integrated Ollama to process unstructured CTI web data. The LLM generates autonomous search queries and acts as a semantic filter to extract valid SHA256 hashes from cybersecurity blogs and social media feeds, reducing noise and false positives during ingestion.

    \item \textbf{Explainability (Drift Context):} When statistical drift is triggered by ADWIN, the LLM processes the shifted feature distributions to generate a human-readable explanation of the concept drift, which is appended to the system logs.

\end{itemize}



\section{Limitations}

\begin{itemize}

  \item Requires MalwareBazaar API key; Ollama and capa are optional but improve source selection and drift explanations.

  \item Drift detection is inactive during bootstrap; sparse labels can skip verified drift batches.

  \item TESSERACT is skipped when temporal splits are single-class or sample counts are too low.

  \item Steady-state malware still depends heavily on curated intel feeds and MalwareBazaar availability.

\end{itemize}



\section{Conclusion}

AMD-Agent demonstrates a Docker-first continual malware pipeline with explicit drift-triggered retraining and first-class evaluation logging suitable for temporal analysis. Future work includes LangGraph subgraphs, broader bootstrap provider rotation, and tighter integration of LLM tool calls with ingest actions.





